course: dictionary
lesson: 4694
title: Homophones: same sound, different spelling
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A homophone is a word that sounds the same as another word but has a different meaning, and often a different spelling. Say them aloud and you cannot tell them apart. Only the spelling and the meaning show which is which.
explain: The words around a homophone, its context, tell you which spelling to choose. «I can see the sea from the top of the hill.» See is what you do with your eyes, and sea is the big salty water. Both sound alike, so think about the meaning before you write.
explain: Learn homophones in pairs or groups, inside sentences. Try the spelling in the sentence and check it makes sense. Ask yourself: does this word mean what I want? If not, try the other spelling.
fact: A homophone sounds the same as another word but has a different meaning.
fact: Homophones are often spelled differently: see and sea, bear and bare, piece and peace.
fact: Two or more such words are called homophones of each other.
fact: Some pairs only sound nearly the same; these are called near-homophones.
fact: Homophones are used in jokes and puns, where one sound has two meanings.
exercise: Which word sounds the same as «sea» but is spelled differently? | *see | sun | salt
exercise: «The bear had ___ paws after the snow melted.» Which word fits? | bear | *bare | barn
exercise: «She ate a big ___ of cake.» Which word fits? | peace | *piece | peas
test: Which homophone of «sun» fits? «My ___ is ten years old.» | sign | *son | soon
test: Which homophone of «night» fits? «The brave ___ rode out of the castle.» | knit | night | *knight
exam: Which pair are homophones? | happy and glad | run and walk | cat and dog | *see and sea
